1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,600
Hi, I'm Edwin.
Today I'll explain seafloor spreading rates.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,800
The half-spreading rate is how fast
one plate moves away from the ridge.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,000
The full rate is how fast the two plates separate:
twice the half-rate if spreading is symmetric.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,200
How do we measure it?
With magnetic stripes.

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,000
The stripes are mirrored on both sides,
and distance divided by age gives the rate.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,500
This cardboard is the ridge, and the strips
are the seafloor on each side.

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,000
As I pull them out, new paper appears in the middle,
like new crust forming at the ridge.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,500
As it cools, the rock records
the direction of Earth's magnetic field.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,500
The stripes match on both sides.

10
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,500
Say this stripe is 100 km from the ridge
and 5 million years old.

11
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,500
100 km ÷ 5 million years
= a half-spreading rate of 2 cm per year.

12
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,800
Let's see the whole process in animation.

13
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,400
At mid-ocean ridges, the plates separate,

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,200
and rising magma forms new oceanic crust.

15
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,400
As the new crust cools,
it records Earth's magnetic field,

16
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,800
producing the same symmetric stripes as my model.
